<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Launch Radar v9 — every pre-token protocol, ranked by what its launch would be worth</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="app.css">
</head>
<body>
<header class="masthead">
  <div class="masthead-inner">
    <div>
      <h1>Launch Radar <span class="version">v9</span></h1>
      <p class="tagline">Every protocol with no token yet, ranked by what its launch would be worth — and the steps to farm it.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="model-line">log<sub>10</sub>(mcap) = 3.5644 + 0.45 &times; log<sub>10</sub>(TVL) + sector + mults</p>
  </div>
  <dl class="kpis" id="kpis"></dl>
</header>

<main>
  <section class="fit" id="fit">
    <h2>How the numbers are fitted — and how far they miss</h2>
    <p>The model is a regression, not a multiple. It is fitted on the 119 live-token protocols in this snapshot (Huber IRLS, c = 1.345, 25 rounds; slope fixed at 0.45; sector offsets are shrunk medians of the live residuals, k = 20, capped at &plusmn;0.3; the growth, maturity and chain multipliers are half-damped). Every term below is measured on this entry, by the scheme named next to it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Leave-one-out RMSE 0.9001</strong> — refit 119 times, one live protocol held out each time (D1 scheme).</li>
      <li><strong>Repeated 5-fold 0.8996</strong> (max over seeds 0, 1, 2; split by shuffled position mod 5) — the other two seeds give 0.8980 and 0.8977 (D2 scheme).</li>
      <li><strong>Walk-forward on the 35 newest launches: median miss &times;2.03</strong> — the model is fitted on launches before each one and scored on the next (D3 scheme).</li>
      <li><strong>Category-blind &times;3.33</strong> — the sector term dropped and the model refit, so a protocol's own category never helps it (D4 scheme).</li>
      <li><strong>Ranges honest:</strong> 50% of launches land in the central 50% band (stated 50&ndash;60%) and 81% in the 10&ndash;90% band (stated 70&ndash;80%).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="fit-note">The model is wrong by about &times;3.3 on a typical launch. The board ranks by the fitted value anyway, because the same error applies to every row: the ranking is the model's best guess, not a promise.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="controls" id="controls">
    <label class="search">Search <input id="q" type="search" placeholder="protocol, category, chain&hellip;" autocomplete="off"></label>
    <label class="check"><input id="watchOnly" type="checkbox"> Watchlist only (40)</label>
    <label class="select">Category
      <select id="cat"><option value="">All categories</option></select>
    </label>
    <label class="select">Sort
      <select id="sort">
        <option value="rank">Rank (implied value)</option>
        <option value="net">Net (conditional)</option>
        <option value="tvl">TVL</option>
        <option value="name">Name</option>
      </select>
    </label>
    <p class="count" id="count"></p>
  </section>

  <section class="board-wrap" id="boardWrap">
    <table id="board">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="num">Rank</th>
          <th>Protocol</th>
          <th>Category</th>
          <th>Chains</th>
          <th class="num">TVL</th>
          <th class="num">7d</th>
